// SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
//! End-to-end coverage of the deterministic replay ENGINE.
//!
//! There are no pre-recorded bags with scheduler traces yet, so every test
//! CRAFTS a bag from a REFERENCE RUN: a small `#[cerulion_node]` graph is run
//! in-process on the polled `GraphRuntime::step()` seam over an isolated
//! per-test transport; its published frames + scheduler trace are captured and
//! written to an MCAP bag (via `cerulion_bag::BagWriter`); then the bag is
//! REPLAYED through `replay_engine::run_engine` with in-process factories (the
//! [`ReplayNodes::Injected`] test seam) and the outcome asserted against a HAND
//! oracle or a crafted perturbation — never a self-compare.
//!
//! The headline contract (Principle #7, Replay = Live): the replayed frames
//! come out BYTE-IDENTICAL to the recording because the engine retraces the
//! recorded gating-clock trajectory (the recorded-clock re-advance). The recorded
//! side is anchored to a hand oracle (`x == counter`, `x == counter + 100`), so
//! byte-equality of the replayed side is a real cross-check, not a tautology.
//!
//! The WHOLE binary is serialized: every `#[test]` carries `#[serial]`. The
//! env-mutating tests serialize for the process-global environment. The
//! transport-touching tests serialize because the binary is empirically racy
//! under default parallelism on a fresh tree (2 failures:
//! EventOpenError(DoesNotExist) / ListenerCreateError; green under
//! `--test-threads=1`). The mechanism is
//! NOT service-name collision: each test mints its OWN iceoryx2 namespace
//! (`iceoryx_test_config()` = `generate_isolated_config()` per call), so
//! same-prefix graphs (e.g. `sr`) in different tests are differently
//! namespaced. The contention is in shared state BELOW the namespace layer
//! (unattributed — suspects: the shared iceoryx2 root/discovery directory,
//! global process state in the transport stack). `#[serial]` is the
//! conservative blanket remedy; only a root-cause attribution would let the
//! non-transport tests run in parallel.

